-- allium: 3
-- compaction.allium
--
-- Merges small tries into larger ones for better query performance.
-- The consumer of this work is the scan operator.
-- It reads the trie catalog to understand which files it has to read for a given query.
--
-- This assumes understanding of the "Building a Bitemporal Index" trilogy:
-- https://xtdb.com/blog/building-a-bitemp-index-1-taxonomy
--
-- Key design constraints:
--
--   Coordination-free: nodes compact collaboratively without coordination.
--   Both job selection and output must be deterministic.
--   Two nodes seeing the same trie catalog must choose the same jobs and produce identical output files.
--   Nodes may be behind others in terms of what files they know to be available.
--   All possible intermediate states must be valid.
--
--   Minimal re-work: jobs should be fine-grained enough that nodes will likely not choose the same job.
--
--   Consistent file sizes: output files target ~100MB.
--   The scan operator doesn't have to handle files of wildly different sizes.
--
--   Clean replacement: once the output file has been written, the input files no longer need to be read.
--   We avoid needing to figure out which _parts_ of a file have been compacted.
--
-- This spec describes job selection and the compaction algorithm only.
-- The message flow (how compaction is triggered and how results are registered)
-- lives in db.allium.
--
-- See: compactor/Compactor.kt

use "./trie-cat.allium" as trie_cat

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

external entity ObjectStore {
    -- Durable key-value storage for blocks, tries, table-blocks.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Job Selection Strategy
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The strategy is staged.
-- Each stage has different trigger conditions:
--
-- == L0 → L1C + L1H (immediately) ==
--
-- As soon as an L0 file is available:
--   Inputs:  the L0 file + a partial L1C file (if one exists, i.e. < 100MB)
--   Outputs: a new L1C for events with recency = ∞
--            0..N L1H files for events with finite recency, per weekly partition
--
-- This is where the current/historical split happens.
-- Events are routed by their recency as determined during bitemporal resolution.
--
-- The L1H files are nascent until the corresponding L1C is uploaded.
-- At that point the previous L1C and the L0 are garbage.
--
-- In practice:
--   'User profile' data has little churn → L1C fills up, compacts to L2C.
--   'Readings' data with user-specified valid-to → L1C stays empty.
--
-- == L1H → L2H (per recency partition) ==
--
-- Triggered when a recency partition has 4 available L1H files,
-- or when the total size of partial L2H + L1H files exceeds 100MB.
--   Inputs:  partial L2H (if exists) + up to 4 L1H files (3 if partial L2H)
--   Output:  a new L2H superseding the partial and the inputs
--
-- == L1C → L2C (when full) ==
--
-- Triggered when 4 full (> 100MB) L1C files exist.
--   Inputs:  the 4 L1C files
--   Outputs: 4 L2C files, one per IID partition (2-bit segments)
--
-- The L2C files are nascent until all 4 partitions are uploaded.
-- Once the fourth is uploaded, all 4 L2C become live, all 4 L1C become garbage.
--
-- == LnC → L(n+1)C / LnH → L(n+1)H (general case) ==
--
-- 4 files in the same partition → next level.
-- L3C partitioned by 2 IID segments (e.g. p13), L4C by 3, etc.
-- Same pattern for historical side.
-- No 'is full' check needed at L2+ — files are assumed to stay ~100MB.

deferred available_jobs(trie_catalog: trie_cat/TrieCatalog) -> List<CompactionJob>
    -- compaction job selection algorithm

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- CompactionJob is ephemeral (coroutine-scoped, not persisted).

entity CompactionJob {
    table: trie_cat/TableRef
    input_trie_keys: List<trie_cat/TrieKey>
    output_tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- TODO(#6263): goes with withinFileRecencyBranching
    -- See: api/CompactorConfig.kt
    -- `compactor.withinFileRecencyBranching`, or env `XTDB_WITHIN_FILE_RECENCY_BRANCHING`.
    -- Whether this node's compactor writes `_recency` into its historical outputs.
    within_file_recency_branching: Boolean = false
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Compaction Algorithm
------------------------------------------------------------

-- See: compactor/Compactor.kt
--
-- Each compaction job yields one or more output segments.
-- Each output segment consists of a data file and a meta file.
--
-- Writing the data file has two stages.
-- Intermediate results are written to disk between stages to bound memory usage.
--
-- == Stage 1: Merge pages to disk ==
--
-- Similarly to the scan operator, we calculate a list of merge tasks.
-- An input page from an unresolved segment (an L0 file, or the live index) first has its same-system-time events resolved against each other.
-- Their order holds only within their page, and the recency split separates them into different files.
-- For each merge task, we merge-sort the input pages by IID and system-time, applying bitemporal resolution.
--
-- The behaviour differs depending on the compaction type:
--   L0 → L1C/L1H: recency is calculated for each event through bitemporal resolution.
--     Events with recency = ∞ go to the current output file.
--     Events with finite recency are routed to historical output files, partitioned into weekly buckets.
--     With `within_file_recency_branching` on, every row of a historical output file carries the recency computed for it in a `_recency` column; current output files carry none.
--   All other compactions: recency from the input file's trie key is preserved into a single output file.
--     Recency is never recomputed above L1H.
--     With `within_file_recency_branching` on, a historical output carries `_recency` through only when every input file carries it; otherwise it carries none.
--   With `within_file_recency_branching` off, no output file carries `_recency`.
--
-- At the end of each merge task (i.e. for each IID path prefix), accumulated rows are flushed to a temporary file on disk.
-- This bounds the memory usage of the compactor.
--
-- == Stage 2: Normalise page sizes ==
--
-- We read the temporary files and write the final output files, normalising page sizes along the way.
-- The input is a tree structure representing the pages written during stage 1, along with their row counts.
--
-- For each node in the tree:
--   If a leaf has more rows than the page size: split by re-partitioning rows by IID.
--     This recurses until each leaf fits within the page size limit.
--   If a branch has fewer rows than the page size: coalesce all descendant leaves into a single page.
--
-- In a file whose data carries `_recency`, a node may branch on recency instead of IID, on the schedule RecencyBranchSchedule sets out.
-- More than a page of versions of one entity can't split on IID: in a file without `_recency` they stay one page.
--
-- This ensures output pages are consistently sized (~1024 rows), regardless of input distribution.
--
-- == Upload ==
--
-- The data file is uploaded first.
-- The presence of the meta file is the marker that the segment is complete.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract OutputSegmentLayout {
    -- What a compaction job's output data and meta files hold, and how the meta file's trie branches.
    -- See: compactor/OutWriter.kt, compactor/SegmentMerge.kt, trie/PageTrieWriter.kt, trie/RecencyRange.kt, trie/MetadataFileWriter.kt

    @invariant HistoricalRowsCarryComputedRecency
        -- With `within_file_recency_branching` on, a job that partitions by recency (L0 → L1C + L1H) writes, for every row of each historical (L1H) output, the recency the merge computes for that row into a `_recency` column.
        -- Its current (L1C) output carries no `_recency` column.

    @invariant RecencyCarriedOnlyWhenEveryInputCarriesIt
        -- With `within_file_recency_branching` on, a job over historical inputs that preserves recency (L1H → L2H and up) copies `_recency` through only when every input file carries it.
        -- Otherwise its output carries none.
        -- Recency is never recomputed above L1H.

    @invariant NoRecencyWrittenWhenBranchingOff
        -- With `within_file_recency_branching` off, no output file carries `_recency`: an L0 → L1C + L1H job writes none, and a historical merge drops it even when every input carries it.
        -- The output is then identical to that of a compactor that never writes `_recency`.

    @invariant FilesReadableWhateverTheSetting
        -- Every node reads files with and without `_recency`, whatever its `within_file_recency_branching` setting.

    @invariant RecencyBranchSchedule
        -- A file whose data carries `_recency` branches its trie on recency as well as IID.
        -- Below the IID levels fixed by the file's part, and at least the first IID level, IID and recency branches alternate, IID first, with no limit on the number of recency levels.
        -- A node on a recency turn whose rows span more than one recency splits the observed recency range of its rows, from their minimum to their maximum, into k parts of equal width.
        -- k is 4 when the node holds more than 4 pages of rows, and otherwise the number of pages its rows fill.
        -- Each non-empty part becomes a child, keyed by the largest recency in it, an inclusive upper bound.
        -- A node on a recency turn whose rows all share one recency branches on IID instead.
        -- A file whose data carries no `_recency` branches on IID only.

    @invariant BranchOnlyAboveAPage
        -- A node branches, on IID or recency, only when it holds more than a page of rows.

    @invariant OneEntityKeepsSplittingOnRecency
        -- A node holding more than a page of versions of one entity can't branch on IID, so it branches on recency as RecencyBranchSchedule sets out, whether or not it is on a recency turn, until its parts fit a page.
        -- Only when its rows all share one recency does it split by count, into pages of roughly equal size, each no larger than a page and keyed by the most recent row in it.
        -- Two such pages may share a key.

    @invariant RecencyBranchNodeOnlyWithRecency
        -- The meta file's trie schema includes the `branch-recency` node type only when the data carries `_recency`.
        -- A file without `_recency` keeps the earlier meta schema.

    @invariant PageMetadataRecordsRecency
        -- In a file whose data carries `_recency`, each page's metadata records the minimum and maximum `_recency` of its rows, alongside the other columns' metadata.
        -- A file whose data carries no `_recency` records none.

    @invariant OutputDeterministic
        -- Every output file, including its recency column and its trie, is a deterministic function of the job and the `within_file_recency_branching` setting.
        -- Every node compacting a database has the same setting, so every node that runs the job writes identical files.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Should compaction have back-pressure when too many jobs are queued?"
